Here's an implementation of the binary search algorithm in Java:
```
public static int binarySearch(int[] arr, int target) {
    int left = 0;
    int right = arr.length - 1;
    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
        if (arr[mid] == target) {
            return mid;
        } else if (arr[mid] < target) {
            left = mid + 1;
        } else {
            right = mid - 1;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
```
The time complexity of the binary search algorithm is O(log n) in the worst case, where n is the size of the array. This is because the algorithm halves the search space at each iteration, so the number of iterations required is proportional to log n.
The best case scenario occurs when the target element is located at the middle of the array, in which case the algorithm returns immediately with a time complexity of O(1).
To optimize the performance of the binary search algorithm, you can use a technique called interpolation search. This involves using the value of the target element to estimate its position in the array, rather than always dividing the search space in half. This can result in faster search times for large, uniformly distributed arrays.
One real-world application of the binary search algorithm is in searching for a particular item in a database or file system. For example, a search engine may use the binary search algorithm to quickly find a particular webpage in its index.
