To implement an optimized and efficient program in C++ to sort an array of integers in descending order using the quicksort algorithm, you can follow these steps:
1. Define a partition function that selects a pivot element and partitions the array into two sub-arrays, one with elements greater than the pivot and one with elements less than or equal to the pivot. This function should return the index of the pivot element.
2. Define a quicksort function that recursively sorts the two sub-arrays using the partition function. The base case for the recursion should be an array with one or zero elements.
3. To handle edge cases, you can add checks for empty arrays or arrays with only one element in the quicksort function.
4. To track the number of comparisons made during the sorting process, you can add a counter variable that increments each time a comparison is made.
Here's an example implementation of the quicksort function in C++ with the additional features:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;
int partition(vector<int>& arr, int low, int high, int& comparisons) {
    int pivot = arr[high];
    int i = low - 1;
    for (int j = low; j < high; j++) {
        comparisons++;
        if (arr[j] >= pivot) {
            i++;
            swap(arr[i], arr[j]);
        }
    }
    swap(arr[i + 1], arr[high]);
    return i + 1;
}
void quicksort(vector<int>& arr, int low, int high, int& comparisons) {
    if (low < high) {
        int pi = partition(arr, low, high, comparisons);
        quicksort(arr, low, pi - 1, comparisons);
        quicksort(arr, pi + 1, high, comparisons);
    }
}
void sortDescending(vector<int>& arr) {
    int comparisons = 0;
    quicksort(arr, 0, arr.size() - 1, comparisons);
    cout << "Number of comparisons: " << comparisons << endl;
}
int main() {
    vector<int> arr = { 5, 2, 9, 3, 7, 6, 8, 1, 4 };
    sortDescending(arr);
    for (int i = 0; i < arr.size(); i++) {
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
In this implementation, the `partition` function selects the last element of the array as the pivot and partitions the array into two sub-arrays. The `quicksort` function recursively sorts the sub-arrays using the `partition` function. The `sortDescending` function calls `quicksort` and also prints the number of comparisons made during the sorting process. The `main` function initializes an array and calls `sortDescending` to sort the array in descending order. Finally, the sorted array is printed to the console.
Note that the `comparisons` variable is passed by reference to the `partition` function and is incremented each time a comparison is made. This allows us to track the number of comparisons made during the sorting process.
This implementation should be efficient for most cases, but there are other optimizations that can be made depending on the specific use case.

