To incorporate the impacts of changing climatic conditions, melting sea ice effects, and breeding and migration pattern alterations on a given ecosystem's animal population through Shell command in C++, we can use a mathematical model that simulates the population dynamics of the animals. The model can be programmed to take into account the various factors that influence population growth and decline, such as birth rates, death rates, immigration, and emigration.
Here is a sample code for modeling the effects of these factors on animal populations in C++:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;
// Define the parameters for the population model
const double r = 0.1;  // Growth rate
const double K = 1000; // Carrying capacity
const double T = 100;  // Simulation time
const double dt = 0.1; // Time step
// Define the initial conditions for the population
double N = 500; // Initial population size
// Define the functions for calculating the birth and death rates
double birth_rate(double N) {
    return r * N * (1 - (N / K));
}
double death_rate(double N) {
    return r * N;
}
// Define the main simulation loop
int main() {
    double t = 0; // Initialize the time variable
    while (t < T) {
        // Calculate the birth and death rates for the current population size
        double B = birth_rate(N);
        double D = death_rate(N);
        // Calculate the new population size using the Euler method
        N = N + (B - D) * dt;
        // Update the time variable
        t = t + dt;
        // Print the current population size and time
        cout << t << "\t" << N << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
To account for genetic diversity's impact on population viability, we can modify the birth and death rate functions to include a term for the genetic diversity of the population. For example:
```c++
// Define the parameters for the population model
const double r = 0.1;  // Growth rate
const double K = 1000; // Carrying capacity
const double T = 100;  // Simulation time
const double dt = 0.1; // Time step
// Define the initial conditions for the population
double N = 500; // Initial population size
double G = 1;   // Initial genetic diversity
// Define the functions for calculating the birth and death rates
double birth_rate(double N, double G) {
    return r * N * (1 - (N / K)) * (1 + G);
}
double death_rate(double N, double G) {
    return r * N * (1 - G);
}
// Define the main simulation loop
int main() {
    double t = 0; // Initialize the time variable
    while (t < T) {
        // Calculate the birth and death rates for the current population size and genetic diversity
        double B = birth_rate(N, G);
        double D = death_rate(N, G);
        // Calculate the new population size and genetic diversity using the Euler method
        N = N + (B - D) * dt;
        G = G + (0.01 * N * G) * dt; // Assume a mutation rate of 0.01 per individual per generation
        // Update the time variable
        t = t + dt;
        // Print the current population size, genetic diversity, and time
        cout << t << "\t" << N << "\t" << G << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This modified code includes a term for genetic diversity in the birth and death rate functions and simulates the mutation rate of the population using the Euler method.

