How can we use C++ to find the number of unique elements in an array?
One possible solution is to use a set to store the elements, as sets only store unique elements. Here's an example code snippet:
int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 4, 5};
int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);
std::set<int> s(arr, arr+n);
int numUnique = s.size();
std::cout << "The number of unique elements in the array is: " << numUnique << std::endl;
Is there a more efficient way to find the number of unique elements in an array using C++?

output:
Another possible solution is to use a hash table to store the frequency of each element in the array. Then, we can count the number of elements with frequency 1 to get the number of unique elements. Here's an example code snippet:
int arr[] = {1, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 4, 5};
int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);
std::unordered_map<int, int> freq;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    freq[arr[i]]++;
}
int numUnique = 0;
for (auto it = freq.begin(); it != freq.end(); it++) {
    if (it->second == 1) {
        numUnique++;
    }
}
std::cout << "The number of unique elements in the array is: " << numUnique << std::endl;
Using a hash table has a time complexity of O(n), which is more efficient than using a set, which has a time complexity of O(n log n) due to the underlying binary search tree.

