How can I efficiently calculate the average price of a set of items in a JSON format using C++ code? Can you suggest a method to extract the prices from the JSON data without using any third-party libraries or APIs? Additionally, how can we handle the case when the JSON data contains millions of items? Please provide an optimized solution that can handle such large data sets efficiently. Here is the JSON data that needs to be processed:
{
  "items": [
    {
      "name": "Item A",
      "price": 28
    },
    {
      "name": "Item B",
      "price": 39
    },
    {
      "name": "Item C",
      "price": 22
    },
    {
      "name": "Item D",
      "price": 27
    }
  ]
}

output:
To extract the prices from the JSON data in C++, you can use the built-in JSON parser in the C++ standard library. Here's an example code snippet that shows how to extract the prices from the JSON data and calculate their average:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <sstream>
#include <vector>
#include <cmath>
#include <json/json.h>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    // Read the JSON data from a file
    ifstream ifs("data.json");
    stringstream buffer;
    buffer << ifs.rdbuf();
    ifs.close();
    string json_str = buffer.str();
    // Parse the JSON data
    Json::Value root;
    Json::Reader reader;
    bool parsingSuccessful = reader.parse(json_str, root);
    if (!parsingSuccessful) {
        cout << "Failed to parse JSON data" << endl;
        return 1;
    }
    // Extract the prices from the JSON data
    vector<double> prices;
    for (auto item : root["items"]) {
        prices.push_back(item["price"].asDouble());
    }
    // Calculate the average price
    double sum = 0;
    for (auto price : prices) {
        sum += price;
    }
    double avg = sum / prices.size();
    cout << "Average price: " << avg << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
To handle the case when the JSON data contains millions of items, you can use a streaming JSON parser, such as the one provided by the RapidJSON library. This allows you to parse the JSON data in chunks, rather than loading the entire data into memory at once. Here's an example code snippet that shows how to use RapidJSON to extract the prices from the JSON data and calculate their average:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <vector>
#include <cmath>
#include "rapidjson/document.h"
#include "rapidjson/istreamwrapper.h"
using namespace std;
using namespace rapidjson;
int main() {
    // Read the JSON data from a file
    ifstream ifs("data.json");
    IStreamWrapper isw(ifs);
    // Parse the JSON data in chunks
    Document doc;
    doc.ParseStream(isw);
    // Extract the prices from the JSON data
    vector<double> prices;
    for (auto& item : doc["items"].GetArray()) {
        prices.push_back(item["price"].GetDouble());
    }
    // Calculate the average price
    double sum = 0;
    for (auto price : prices) {
        sum += price;
    }
    double avg = sum / prices.size();
    cout << "Average price: " << avg << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
Using a streaming JSON parser allows you to process large JSON data sets efficiently, without running out of memory.

