One way to check if three strings have a common letter in Java is to iterate through each character of the first string and check if it appears in the other two strings. Here's an example code:
```java
public class CommonLetterFinder {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str1 = "Apple";
        String str2 = "Orange";
        String str3 = "Carrot";
        boolean hasCommonLetter = false;
        for (int i = 0; i < str1.length(); i++) {
            char c = str1.charAt(i);
            if (str2.indexOf(c) != -1 && str3.indexOf(c) != -1) {
                System.out.println("The common letter is: " + c);
                hasCommonLetter = true;
                break;
            }
        }
        if (!hasCommonLetter) {
            System.out.println("There is no common letter.");
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, we first initialize three strings `str1`, `str2`, and `str3`. We also initialize a boolean variable `hasCommonLetter` to keep track of whether we have found a common letter or not.
We then loop through each character of `str1` using a for loop and check if the character appears in both `str2` and `str3` using the `indexOf` method. If we find a common letter, we output it and set `hasCommonLetter` to true. We also break out of the loop since we only need to find one common letter.
If we don't find any common letters after iterating through all characters of `str1`, we output that there is no common letter.

