To prevent division by zero errors, you can add a check to ensure that the humidity parameter is not equal to zero before performing the calculation. Here is the modified code with error handling:
public string celsiusToFahrenheit(double celsius, double pressure, double humidity) {
  if (humidity == 0) {
    return "Error: Humidity cannot be zero";
  }
  double fahrenheit = (celsius * 9/5) + 32;
  double adjustedFahrenheit = fahrenheit + ((pressure - 1013) / 100) - ((humidity - 50) / 10);
  return Math.Round(adjustedFahrenheit, 2).ToString() + " °F";
}
This code checks if the humidity parameter is equal to zero, and if so, it returns an error message instead of performing the calculation. This ensures that the function does not encounter a division by zero error.

